1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,297
Computational Skepticism, Exam Prep.

2
00:00:01,347 --> 00:00:02,644
Key Terms: Fairness Metrics.

3
00:00:02,694 --> 00:00:07,022
Thirty-one terms for saying what fair
means for a yes-or-no model, and for

4
00:00:07,072 --> 00:00:09,379
showing why arithmetic alone cannot settle
it.

5
00:00:09,429 --> 00:00:14,430
With them you can name which fairness you
are claiming, and which you gave up.

6
00:00:15,580 --> 00:00:15,798
Hallo.

7
00:00:15,848 --> 00:00:17,406
This is Liam, in for Bear.

8
00:00:17,456 --> 00:00:20,890
A newcomer thinks that which fairness
metric to use is a technical question,

9
00:00:20,940 --> 00:00:21,962
something the math settles.

10
00:00:22,012 --> 00:00:22,766
It does not.

11
00:00:22,816 --> 00:00:26,250
When groups differ in how often the
outcome is truly positive, the reasonable

12
00:00:26,300 --> 00:00:29,734
definitions cannot all hold, so picking
one is a choice you must defend.

13
00:00:29,784 --> 00:00:32,950
This film goes through the terms for that
choice, each one shown.

14
00:00:33,300 --> 00:00:36,272
Six labels sit in the corners of the
pictures that follow.

15
00:00:36,322 --> 00:00:39,569
In the book text: the course text uses and
explains the term.

16
00:00:39,619 --> 00:00:44,239
In the glossary: the term is on the course
term list, but the text does not explain

17
00:00:44,289 --> 00:00:44,513
it.

18
00:00:44,563 --> 00:00:47,535
Computed: the numbers are worked out on
screen from stated inputs.

19
00:00:47,585 --> 00:00:51,931
Simulation: a made-up run with a fixed
seed, so it comes out the same every time.

20
00:00:51,981 --> 00:00:54,678
Illustrative data: numbers chosen to show
the idea, not measured.

21
00:00:54,728 --> 00:00:57,700
Conceptual: a drawing of the idea, with no
data behind it.

22
00:00:58,050 --> 00:01:01,423
This is a map of thirty-one terms in four
groups.

23
00:01:01,473 --> 00:01:05,873
The first group, Definitions and the
theorem, holds base rate, the error rates,

24
00:01:05,923 --> 00:01:09,296
and three yardsticks of fairness,
demographic parity, equalized odds and

25
00:01:09,346 --> 00:01:09,980
calibration parity.

26
00:01:10,030 --> 00:01:14,430
The impossibility theorem says those three
cannot all hold when base rates differ.

27
00:01:14,780 --> 00:01:18,530
The second group, Choosing and defending,
is about what the choice means.

28
00:01:18,580 --> 00:01:20,113
COMPAS is the public case.

29
00:01:20,163 --> 00:01:22,647
A values claim is what each metric
carries.

30
00:01:22,697 --> 00:01:25,180
A defended choice is what you hand in.

31
00:01:25,230 --> 00:01:28,663
Pre-processing, in-processing and post-
processing are the tools that carry it

32
00:01:28,713 --> 00:01:28,980
out.

33
00:01:29,330 --> 00:01:33,455
The third group, Beyond groups, moves from
whole groups to single people.

34
00:01:33,505 --> 00:01:37,283
It holds group fairness and individual
fairness, the Lipschitz condition, the

35
00:01:37,333 --> 00:01:39,718
similarity metric d, awareness,
unawareness and proxy.

36
00:01:39,768 --> 00:01:42,154
The choice does not go away here.

37
00:01:42,204 --> 00:01:42,850
It moves.

38
00:01:43,200 --> 00:01:48,649
The fourth group, Causal fairness and the
GE Index, is about paths and one summary

39
00:01:48,699 --> 00:01:49,015
number.

40
00:01:49,065 --> 00:01:53,048
It holds the structural causal model,
direct effect, indirect effect, spurious

41
00:01:53,098 --> 00:01:57,080
effect and counterfactual fairness, then
benefit, the GE Index, short for

42
00:01:57,130 --> 00:01:59,280
generalized entropy index, alpha and
decomposition.

43
00:01:59,630 --> 00:02:01,436
Two tables of two hundred people each.

44
00:02:01,486 --> 00:02:04,087
The top row, outlined in red, is everyone
truly positive.

45
00:02:04,137 --> 00:02:08,594
Their share is the base rate, zero point
six in Group A and zero point three in

46
00:02:08,644 --> 00:02:10,185
Group B in this worked example.

47
00:02:10,235 --> 00:02:13,631
For example, a résumé screener sees two
applicant pools for a data analyst

48
00:02:13,681 --> 00:02:13,897
opening.

49
00:02:13,947 --> 00:02:18,669
The share in each pool who would truly do
well in the job is that pool's base rate,

50
00:02:18,719 --> 00:02:21,320
and the two shares can differ before any
model runs.

51
00:02:21,670 --> 00:02:23,108
Stay inside that top row.

52
00:02:23,158 --> 00:02:27,272
The true-positive rate is the share of the
truly positive that the model catches.

53
00:02:27,322 --> 00:02:31,140
In Group A, seventy-two caught and forty-
eight missed gives zero point six.

54
00:02:31,190 --> 00:02:33,520
In Group B it is zero point seven.

55
00:02:33,570 --> 00:02:37,090
For example, take the applicants who
really are qualified for a role.

56
00:02:37,140 --> 00:02:40,660
The true-positive rate is the share of
them an applicant-tracking system passes

57
00:02:40,710 --> 00:02:41,850
on to the recruiter.

58
00:02:41,900 --> 00:02:44,230
Every qualified person it screens out
lowers it.

59
00:02:44,580 --> 00:02:47,553
Now the bottom row, people who are truly
not positive.

60
00:02:47,603 --> 00:02:51,180
The false-positive rate is the share of
them the model flags anyway.

61
00:02:51,230 --> 00:02:54,807
In Group A, twenty-four false alarms
against fifty-six correct calls gives zero

62
00:02:54,857 --> 00:02:55,412
point three.

63
00:02:55,462 --> 00:02:57,830
In Group B it is zero point one.

64
00:02:57,880 --> 00:03:01,760
For example, picture an agent that tells a
job seeker which listings fit.

65
00:03:01,810 --> 00:03:06,294
Among the listings that truly do not fit,
the false-positive rate is the share it

66
00:03:06,344 --> 00:03:08,410
recommends anyway, each one a wasted
application.

67
00:03:08,760 --> 00:03:11,310
Now the left column, everyone the model
flagged.

68
00:03:11,360 --> 00:03:14,559
Predictive value, or PPV, positive
predictive value, also called precision,

69
00:03:14,609 --> 00:03:19,758
is the share of the flagged who are truly
positive, zero point seven five in both

70
00:03:19,808 --> 00:03:20,083
groups.

71
00:03:20,133 --> 00:03:22,357
True-positive rate starts from the truly
positive.

72
00:03:22,407 --> 00:03:24,307
Predictive value starts from the flagged.

73
00:03:24,357 --> 00:03:28,531
For example, a recruiter looks only at the
shortlist an AI screener produced.

74
00:03:28,581 --> 00:03:32,431
Predictive value is the share of that
shortlist who are truly qualified.

75
00:03:32,481 --> 00:03:35,680
It says nothing about qualified people
left off the list.

76
00:03:36,030 --> 00:03:37,716
Bars for the same two groups.

77
00:03:37,766 --> 00:03:40,609
Demographic parity, also called
statistical parity, asks that the model

78
00:03:40,659 --> 00:03:43,212
says yes at the same rate in every group.

79
00:03:43,262 --> 00:03:47,263
The yes rates here are zero point four
eight and zero point two eight.

80
00:03:47,313 --> 00:03:47,841
Not met.

81
00:03:47,891 --> 00:03:51,313
For example, an interview-prediction model
meets demographic parity if it invites the

82
00:03:51,363 --> 00:03:55,652
same share of older and younger applicants
to interview, whether or not the two pools

83
00:03:55,702 --> 00:03:56,520
are equally qualified.

84
00:03:56,870 --> 00:04:01,409
Equalized odds asks for the same error
rates in every group, both TPR, true-

85
00:04:01,459 --> 00:04:03,376
positive rate, and FPR, false-positive
rate.

86
00:04:03,426 --> 00:04:08,293
Here they are zero point six against zero
point seven, and zero point three against

87
00:04:08,343 --> 00:04:09,277
zero point one.

88
00:04:09,327 --> 00:04:11,899
Neither matches, so equalized odds is not
met.

89
00:04:11,949 --> 00:04:15,505
For example, a résumé screener meets
equalized odds only if qualified

90
00:04:15,555 --> 00:04:19,766
applicants with and without a disability
pass at the same rate, and unqualified

91
00:04:19,816 --> 00:04:23,700
applicants from both groups are wrongly
passed at the same rate too.

92
00:04:24,050 --> 00:04:27,617
Calibration parity, also called predictive
parity, asks that a stated probability

93
00:04:27,667 --> 00:04:30,248
means the same observed rate in every
group.

94
00:04:30,298 --> 00:04:35,838
For a yes or no score that is equal PPV,
zero point seven five in both bars.

95
00:04:35,888 --> 00:04:38,798
One model, calibration parity met, the
other two broken.

96
00:04:38,848 --> 00:04:43,402
For example, a model scores how likely
each applicant is to get an offer.

97
00:04:43,452 --> 00:04:47,348
Calibration parity holds if, among
applicants given the same score, the same

98
00:04:47,398 --> 00:04:49,650
share actually get offers in every group.

99
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:51,852
A short proof in two bars.

100
00:04:51,902 --> 00:04:55,656
The impossibility theorem says that with
unequal base rates, calibration parity and

101
00:04:55,706 --> 00:04:57,241
equalized odds cannot both hold.

102
00:04:57,291 --> 00:05:01,679
Equal precision forces the ratio of true-
positive rate to false-positive rate to be

103
00:05:01,729 --> 00:05:04,533
two for Group A and seven for Group B.

104
00:05:04,583 --> 00:05:06,118
Equalized odds needs one ratio.

105
00:05:06,168 --> 00:05:10,873
For example, if more of one applicant pool
is truly qualified, a hiring screen cannot

106
00:05:10,923 --> 00:05:15,945
keep its yes meaning the same in both
pools and also make its mistakes at the

107
00:05:15,995 --> 00:05:16,579
same rates.

108
00:05:16,629 --> 00:05:17,530
One must give.

109
00:05:17,880 --> 00:05:22,691
The curve is the factor between those two
ratios as the base rate of Group B

110
00:05:22,741 --> 00:05:22,995
changes.

111
00:05:23,045 --> 00:05:27,248
It equals one only at the black dot, where
the base rates are equal.

112
00:05:27,298 --> 00:05:30,286
The only other escape from the
impossibility theorem is perfect

113
00:05:30,336 --> 00:05:30,590
prediction.

114
00:05:30,940 --> 00:05:33,820
Two score curves from an illustrative
model, one per group.

115
00:05:33,870 --> 00:05:38,509
The threshold is the score cutoff above
which the model says yes, the red line at

116
00:05:38,559 --> 00:05:39,388
zero point five.

117
00:05:39,438 --> 00:05:43,197
One threshold for both, and the false-
positive rates still differ, zero point

118
00:05:43,247 --> 00:05:44,955
five against zero point zero nine.

119
00:05:45,005 --> 00:05:48,472
For example, an applicant-tracking system
gives each résumé a score, and only

120
00:05:48,522 --> 00:05:50,523
résumés above the cutoff reach a
recruiter.

121
00:05:50,573 --> 00:05:53,160
Move that cutoff and you change who gets
seen.

122
00:05:53,510 --> 00:05:57,529
The threshold for Group B now drops to
zero point two two.

123
00:05:57,579 --> 00:06:01,597
False-positive rates match at zero point
five, but precision splits, zero point

124
00:06:01,647 --> 00:06:03,970
seven one against zero point four two.

125
00:06:04,020 --> 00:06:06,344
A threshold picks which gap you keep.

126
00:06:06,394 --> 00:06:07,700
It never removes both.

127
00:06:08,050 --> 00:06:10,105
Two bars from a real audit.

128
00:06:10,155 --> 00:06:13,614
COMPAS is a tool that scores the risk of
re-arrest.

129
00:06:13,664 --> 00:06:16,771
ProPublica reported that among people
never re-arrested, about forty-five

130
00:06:16,821 --> 00:06:20,981
percent of Black defendants were labeled
high risk, against about twenty-three and

131
00:06:21,031 --> 00:06:23,087
a half percent of white defendants.

132
00:06:23,137 --> 00:06:24,490
That violates equalized odds.

133
00:06:24,840 --> 00:06:27,307
Ten pairs of bars, one per risk score.

134
00:06:27,357 --> 00:06:31,712
Northpointe, the maker of COMPAS, answered
that each score meant about the same re-

135
00:06:31,762 --> 00:06:33,285
arrest rate in both groups.

136
00:06:33,335 --> 00:06:34,543
That is calibration parity.

137
00:06:34,593 --> 00:06:35,802
The bars are illustrative.

138
00:06:35,852 --> 00:06:37,060
Both claims are true.

139
00:06:37,410 --> 00:06:38,731
Three boxes, one per metric.

140
00:06:38,781 --> 00:06:42,844
A values claim is the belief about who
should bear the errors, carried by the

141
00:06:42,894 --> 00:06:43,667
choice of metric.

142
00:06:43,717 --> 00:06:47,505
Equalized odds says your group should not
change your risk of being wrongly flagged.

143
00:06:47,555 --> 00:06:49,699
Calibration parity protects the meaning of
the score.

144
00:06:49,749 --> 00:06:51,070
None is the obvious one.

145
00:06:51,120 --> 00:06:54,634
For example, a company that picks
equalized odds for its résumé screen is

146
00:06:54,684 --> 00:06:57,651
claiming that being wrongly rejected
should not depend on your group.

147
00:06:57,701 --> 00:06:59,570
That is a value, not a calculation.

148
00:06:59,920 --> 00:07:00,729
Six numbered steps.

149
00:07:00,779 --> 00:07:04,450
A defended choice is the deliverable, a
chosen metric with written reasons, ending

150
00:07:04,500 --> 00:07:06,168
with what would change your mind.

151
00:07:06,218 --> 00:07:07,885
A number alone is not enough.

152
00:07:07,935 --> 00:07:11,606
For COMPAS the court system should have
decided, and said so in writing.

153
00:07:11,656 --> 00:07:15,328
For example, a team running an interview-
prediction model writes down which metric

154
00:07:15,378 --> 00:07:19,621
it chose, why, who pays when it misses a
qualified applicant, and what result would

155
00:07:19,671 --> 00:07:20,480
make it switch.

156
00:07:20,830 --> 00:07:23,450
Eight squares, one per applicant, and
filled means approved.

157
00:07:23,500 --> 00:07:26,714
Group fairness judges a model by comparing
rates across whole groups.

158
00:07:26,764 --> 00:07:31,462
In this made-up example each group has two
of four approved, zero point five each, so

159
00:07:31,512 --> 00:07:32,649
the group check passes.

160
00:07:32,699 --> 00:07:36,803
For example, an employer checks that its
screening agent passes the same share of

161
00:07:36,853 --> 00:07:39,770
each group to the interview stage, and
looks no further.

162
00:07:40,120 --> 00:07:42,806
Two squares are now outlined, applicants x
and y.

163
00:07:42,856 --> 00:07:47,063
Same income, history and debt ratio, yet x
is approved and y is denied.

164
00:07:47,113 --> 00:07:49,799
Individual fairness says similar people
should be treated similarly.

165
00:07:49,849 --> 00:07:52,839
The totals still balance, so a group audit
sees nothing.

166
00:07:52,889 --> 00:07:57,096
For example, two applicants with the same
degree, the same experience and the same

167
00:07:57,146 --> 00:08:01,352
skills should get similar fit scores from
a résumé screener, whatever happens to the

168
00:08:01,402 --> 00:08:01,960
group totals.

169
00:08:02,310 --> 00:08:04,244
The Lipschitz condition makes that a test.

170
00:08:04,294 --> 00:08:07,928
The difference in treatment may not exceed
the distance between the two people.

171
00:08:07,978 --> 00:08:11,895
Here the treatment gap is zero point one
four and the distance, on financial

172
00:08:11,945 --> 00:08:13,878
variables only, is zero point zero two.

173
00:08:13,928 --> 00:08:15,295
So the condition is violated.

174
00:08:15,345 --> 00:08:18,696
For example, if two résumés are nearly
identical, the condition lets their

175
00:08:18,746 --> 00:08:20,396
interview scores differ only a little.

176
00:08:20,446 --> 00:08:24,080
A large gap in scores for a tiny gap in
résumés breaks it.

177
00:08:24,430 --> 00:08:28,943
The similarity metric d measures how alike
two people are, and someone has to choose

178
00:08:28,993 --> 00:08:29,247
it.

179
00:08:29,297 --> 00:08:33,506
Count zip-code distance and it grows to
zero point three two, so the same

180
00:08:33,556 --> 00:08:34,723
treatment gap now passes.

181
00:08:34,773 --> 00:08:35,940
Same equation, opposite guarantee.

182
00:08:35,990 --> 00:08:37,765
Choosing it is a values choice.

183
00:08:37,815 --> 00:08:41,719
For example, a team comparing applicants
must decide whether a gap in employment

184
00:08:41,769 --> 00:08:43,544
history makes two people less alike.

185
00:08:43,594 --> 00:08:46,282
Count it, and the screener may treat them
differently.

186
00:08:46,332 --> 00:08:49,020
Leave it out, and they must be treated
alike.

187
00:08:49,370 --> 00:08:54,393
A table of what the model is given, with
the race column boxed in red and marked

188
00:08:54,443 --> 00:08:54,691
removed.

189
00:08:54,741 --> 00:08:57,675
Unawareness, or FTU, fairness through
unawareness, means dropping the sensitive

190
00:08:57,725 --> 00:08:59,465
attribute and calling the model fair.

191
00:08:59,515 --> 00:09:01,554
Notice the zip column is still there.

192
00:09:01,604 --> 00:09:06,029
For example, a vendor strips the name and
gender fields from every résumé before its

193
00:09:06,079 --> 00:09:09,610
screener scores them, and calls the
screener fair for that reason alone.

194
00:09:09,960 --> 00:09:13,574
Two bars show who is in each zip code, in
a simulated run.

195
00:09:13,624 --> 00:09:16,392
A proxy is a feature that reveals group
membership indirectly.

196
00:09:16,442 --> 00:09:20,338
Guessing the group from zip code alone is
right eighty-five percent of the time.

197
00:09:20,388 --> 00:09:21,748
Only direct use was blocked.

198
00:09:21,798 --> 00:09:25,975
For example, a résumé screener that never
sees an applicant's age can still work it

199
00:09:26,025 --> 00:09:28,230
out from the year their degree was earned.

200
00:09:28,580 --> 00:09:32,117
Awareness, or FTA, fairness through
awareness, means stating openly how much

201
00:09:32,167 --> 00:09:34,074
group membership counts when judging
similarity.

202
00:09:34,124 --> 00:09:39,291
With a group weight of zero point three,
the distance goes from zero point zero two

203
00:09:39,341 --> 00:09:40,922
to zero point three two.

204
00:09:40,972 --> 00:09:42,879
Uncomfortable, because it names the thing.

205
00:09:42,929 --> 00:09:47,118
For example, the team behind a résumé
screener writes into its rule for

206
00:09:47,168 --> 00:09:51,357
comparing applicants exactly how much
being in a different group adds, so anyone

207
00:09:51,407 --> 00:09:53,640
can see that weight and challenge it.

208
00:09:53,990 --> 00:09:55,528
Four circles joined by arrows.

209
00:09:55,578 --> 00:09:59,656
A structural causal model is exactly this,
variables with arrows for which causes

210
00:09:59,706 --> 00:09:59,973
which.

211
00:10:00,023 --> 00:10:03,466
The red arrow runs straight from the
attribute to the prediction.

212
00:10:03,516 --> 00:10:07,276
That is the direct effect, also called
disparate treatment, strength zero point

213
00:10:07,326 --> 00:10:08,864
three in this made-up example.

214
00:10:08,914 --> 00:10:12,992
For example, a structural causal model of
an interview predictor draws arrows from

215
00:10:13,042 --> 00:10:15,214
age, experience and skills to the score.

216
00:10:15,264 --> 00:10:19,342
If the score drops just because the
applicant is older, nothing else changed,

217
00:10:19,392 --> 00:10:20,930
that is a direct effect.

218
00:10:21,280 --> 00:10:22,904
Now the upper path is red.

219
00:10:22,954 --> 00:10:26,532
The attribute moves a mediator, such as
education, and the mediator moves the

220
00:10:26,582 --> 00:10:26,811
prediction.

221
00:10:26,861 --> 00:10:29,323
That is the indirect effect, also called
disparate impact.

222
00:10:29,373 --> 00:10:33,230
Zero point four times zero point five, so
this path adds zero point two.

223
00:10:33,280 --> 00:10:37,416
For example, if one group has had less
chance to finish a degree, and an

224
00:10:37,466 --> 00:10:40,765
interview model rewards degrees, the group
moves the score through the degree.

225
00:10:40,815 --> 00:10:42,160
That is an indirect effect.

226
00:10:42,510 --> 00:10:45,836
On the lower path the first arrow points
into the attribute.

227
00:10:45,886 --> 00:10:48,599
A confounder drives both the attribute and
the prediction.

228
00:10:48,649 --> 00:10:52,589
That is the spurious effect, a link with
no causal path between them.

229
00:10:52,639 --> 00:10:56,579
Data alone shows only the total of all
three paths, zero point eight.

230
00:10:56,629 --> 00:10:59,035
For example, take first-generation
graduates as the group.

231
00:10:59,085 --> 00:11:03,332
Family income shapes who is a first-
generation graduate, and it also pays for

232
00:11:03,382 --> 00:11:05,481
the unpaid internships a résumé screener
rewards.

233
00:11:05,531 --> 00:11:08,550
Group and score move together with no
arrow between them.

234
00:11:08,900 --> 00:11:10,434
Three boxes follow one person.

235
00:11:10,484 --> 00:11:14,552
Counterfactual fairness asks whether this
same person would get the same decision in

236
00:11:14,602 --> 00:11:15,503
the other group.

237
00:11:15,553 --> 00:11:18,987
Abduction infers their background, action
flips the attribute, prediction runs the

238
00:11:19,037 --> 00:11:19,621
model again.

239
00:11:19,671 --> 00:11:22,789
The score moves by zero point two, so it
fails.

240
00:11:22,839 --> 00:11:25,957
For example, take one applicant an
interview model turned down.

241
00:11:26,007 --> 00:11:29,758
Keep her background, flip only her group,
and run the model again.

242
00:11:29,808 --> 00:11:33,560
If she would now be invited, the model is
not counterfactually fair.

243
00:11:33,910 --> 00:11:37,202
Eight bars, one per person, with a dashed
line at the mean.

244
00:11:37,252 --> 00:11:40,823
Benefit is the number per person whose
spread gets measured, a score, a

245
00:11:40,873 --> 00:11:42,215
probability or an error flag.

246
00:11:42,265 --> 00:11:45,279
In these made-up scores the mean is zero
point five five.

247
00:11:45,329 --> 00:11:48,343
For example, in an interview-prediction
model the benefit could be each

248
00:11:48,393 --> 00:11:51,963
applicant's predicted chance of an
interview, or a flag for whether the model

249
00:11:52,013 --> 00:11:53,077
got that applicant wrong.

250
00:11:53,127 --> 00:11:54,470
Someone has to pick which.

251
00:11:54,820 --> 00:11:58,587
Red lines now mark how far each bar is
from the mean.

252
00:11:58,637 --> 00:12:03,040
The GE Index, generalized entropy index,
is one number for how unequally benefit is

253
00:12:03,090 --> 00:12:05,267
spread across people, as with income
inequality.

254
00:12:05,317 --> 00:12:07,811
Here it is zero point zero six two.

255
00:12:07,861 --> 00:12:09,083
More spread, larger index.

256
00:12:09,133 --> 00:12:13,218
For example, a job-matching agent gives
some users many strong listings and others

257
00:12:13,268 --> 00:12:13,855
almost none.

258
00:12:13,905 --> 00:12:18,308
The GE Index puts one number on how
unevenly those match scores are spread

259
00:12:18,358 --> 00:12:19,580
across all its users.

260
00:12:19,930 --> 00:12:23,758
Alpha is the dial in the GE Index that
sets whose unfairness counts more.

261
00:12:23,808 --> 00:12:26,250
Low alpha weights the bottom, high alpha
the top.

262
00:12:26,300 --> 00:12:30,682
When the lowest benefit falls, the index
rises far more at alpha zero than at alpha

263
00:12:30,732 --> 00:12:30,959
two.

264
00:12:31,009 --> 00:12:34,560
For example, an auditor of a résumé
screener who worries most about the

265
00:12:34,610 --> 00:12:36,498
applicants scored lowest picks a low
alpha.

266
00:12:36,548 --> 00:12:40,653
One who worries about a few applicants
scored far above the rest picks a high

267
00:12:40,703 --> 00:12:40,930
alpha.

268
00:12:41,280 --> 00:12:43,392
The bar is split in two colours.

269
00:12:43,442 --> 00:12:46,788
GE decomposition says total unfairness
equals the within-group part plus the

270
00:12:46,838 --> 00:12:47,406
between-group part.

271
00:12:47,456 --> 00:12:51,112
In this worked example seventy-five
percent sits inside the groups and twenty-

272
00:12:51,162 --> 00:12:52,347
five percent between them.

273
00:12:52,397 --> 00:12:54,817
A group audit sees only the red part.

274
00:12:54,867 --> 00:12:59,449
For example, an audit of a hiring model's
scores finds most of the unevenness among

275
00:12:59,499 --> 00:13:00,993
applicants inside the same group.

276
00:13:01,043 --> 00:13:04,390
Evening out the group averages would leave
most of it untouched.

277
00:13:04,740 --> 00:13:07,712
Three boxes in a row, training data, model
training, predictions.

278
00:13:07,762 --> 00:13:10,733
Pre-processing changes the training data
before training, for example by

279
00:13:10,783 --> 00:13:11,035
reweighting.

280
00:13:11,085 --> 00:13:14,661
Here the positive share becomes zero point
four five in both groups.

281
00:13:14,711 --> 00:13:16,474
That equalizes base rates, not errors.

282
00:13:16,524 --> 00:13:20,402
For example, before an interview predictor
learns from past hiring records, the team

283
00:13:20,452 --> 00:13:24,935
gives extra weight to past hires from a
group that was rarely hired, so the

284
00:13:24,985 --> 00:13:27,050
records it learns from are more balanced.

285
00:13:27,400 --> 00:13:28,632
Now the middle box.

286
00:13:28,682 --> 00:13:31,838
In-processing changes the training
objective, for example by adding a

287
00:13:31,888 --> 00:13:32,479
fairness penalty.

288
00:13:32,529 --> 00:13:36,325
In this illustrative model, a bigger
penalty shrinks the false-positive gap to

289
00:13:36,375 --> 00:13:41,134
zero, while the error rises from zero
point two nine to zero point three six.

290
00:13:41,184 --> 00:13:45,301
For example, a résumé-scoring model is
trained to be accurate, but loses points

291
00:13:45,351 --> 00:13:49,147
during training whenever it wrongly passes
unqualified applicants from one group more

292
00:13:49,197 --> 00:13:50,750
often than from the other.

293
00:13:51,100 --> 00:13:51,982
The last box.

294
00:13:52,032 --> 00:13:55,087
Post-processing leaves the model frozen
and changes predictions afterwards, for

295
00:13:55,137 --> 00:13:56,950
example with a threshold per group.

296
00:13:57,000 --> 00:14:01,297
A lower cutoff for Group B equalizes the
share flagged, but its false-positive rate

297
00:14:01,347 --> 00:14:04,402
goes from zero point zero nine to zero
point six.

298
00:14:04,452 --> 00:14:09,060
For example, a company keeps the résumé
screener it bought exactly as it is, and

299
00:14:09,110 --> 00:14:11,855
only sets a different pass score for each
group.

300
00:14:11,905 --> 00:14:14,960
That needs each applicant's group at the
moment of screening.

301
00:14:15,310 --> 00:14:16,852
Two terms side by side.

302
00:14:16,902 --> 00:14:20,991
Demographic parity compares one thing, the
share of each group that is flagged.

303
00:14:21,041 --> 00:14:24,811
Equalized odds compares two error rates,
and those need the true outcomes.

304
00:14:24,861 --> 00:14:28,950
If a case mentions only who got a yes, it
is demographic parity.

305
00:14:29,300 --> 00:14:32,574
Group fairness on the left, individual
fairness on the right.

306
00:14:32,624 --> 00:14:35,566
Group fairness checks that rates match
across whole groups.

307
00:14:35,616 --> 00:14:39,556
Individual fairness checks, pair by pair,
that similar people get similar treatment.

308
00:14:39,606 --> 00:14:42,880
A model can pass the first and fail the
second.

309
00:14:43,230 --> 00:14:45,790
Awareness on the left, unawareness on the
right.

310
00:14:45,840 --> 00:14:49,379
Awareness names how much group counts in
the similarity metric d.

311
00:14:49,429 --> 00:14:51,336
Unawareness drops the column and hopes.

312
00:14:51,386 --> 00:14:56,230
In the simulated run, zip code still named
the group eighty-five percent of the time.

313
00:14:56,580 --> 00:14:59,874
Disparate treatment on the left, disparate
impact on the right.

314
00:14:59,924 --> 00:15:03,887
Disparate treatment is the direct effect,
where the attribute itself moves the

315
00:15:03,937 --> 00:15:04,221
prediction.

316
00:15:04,271 --> 00:15:08,234
Disparate impact is the indirect effect,
where the attribute acts through a

317
00:15:08,284 --> 00:15:08,568
mediator.

318
00:15:08,618 --> 00:15:10,240
Look for the middle circle.

319
00:15:10,590 --> 00:15:14,608
A vague request with the exact version
under it, and a chart on the right.

320
00:15:14,658 --> 00:15:16,778
Make the model fair is not an instruction.

321
00:15:16,828 --> 00:15:19,219
Report each gap and name what is given up.

322
00:15:19,269 --> 00:15:23,016
Here the precision gap is zero, so
calibration parity is met, and the other

323
00:15:23,066 --> 00:15:23,830
two are not.

324
00:15:24,180 --> 00:15:26,126
It is accurate, so it is fair.

325
00:15:26,176 --> 00:15:27,836
No, accuracy is a different question.

326
00:15:27,886 --> 00:15:32,683
In the worked example the model is right
for zero point six four of Group A and

327
00:15:32,733 --> 00:15:34,679
zero point eight four of Group B.

328
00:15:34,729 --> 00:15:36,390
The overall figure hides that gap.

329
00:15:36,440 --> 00:15:37,530
Report accuracy per group.

330
00:15:37,880 --> 00:15:39,438
Just drop the race column.

331
00:15:39,488 --> 00:15:42,653
That is unawareness, and it fails when a
proxy remains.

332
00:15:42,703 --> 00:15:45,546
The exact instruction is to test the
remaining features.

333
00:15:45,596 --> 00:15:50,369
In the simulated run, zip code alone names
the group right eighty-five percent of the

334
00:15:50,419 --> 00:15:50,690
time.

335
00:15:51,040 --> 00:15:52,300
Treat similar people alike.

336
00:15:52,350 --> 00:15:56,557
That is individual fairness, and it is
empty until you define the similarity

337
00:15:56,607 --> 00:15:57,211
metric d.

338
00:15:57,261 --> 00:16:02,450
In the made-up example the same treatment
gap fails at a distance of zero point zero

339
00:16:02,500 --> 00:16:05,070
two and passes at zero point three two.

340
00:16:05,420 --> 00:16:07,128
How unfair is it overall.

341
00:16:07,178 --> 00:16:12,049
Define the benefit, compute the GE Index,
then split it with the GE decomposition.

342
00:16:12,099 --> 00:16:16,971
In the worked example, zero point zero
four eight sits within groups and zero

343
00:16:17,021 --> 00:16:19,080
point zero one seven between them.

344
00:16:19,430 --> 00:16:23,635
A checklist of six questions to answer
before you defend a fairness metric.

345
00:16:23,685 --> 00:16:27,236
First, which of the three definitions are
you reporting, demographic parity,

346
00:16:27,286 --> 00:16:31,163
equalized odds or calibration parity, and
which two do you give up?

347
00:16:31,213 --> 00:16:32,800
You cannot keep all three.

348
00:16:33,150 --> 00:16:35,945
Second, do the base rates differ, and by
how much?

349
00:16:35,995 --> 00:16:39,644
The base rate is the share of each group
that is truly positive.

350
00:16:39,694 --> 00:16:43,058
If the base rates are equal, the
impossibility theorem does not bite.

351
00:16:43,108 --> 00:16:45,050
If they differ, a trade is forced.

352
00:16:45,400 --> 00:16:48,469
Third, who bears the cost of each kind of
error here?

353
00:16:48,519 --> 00:16:51,588
A false alarm and a missed case fall on
different people.

354
00:16:51,638 --> 00:16:54,990
Answering this is how you find the values
claim behind your metric.

355
00:16:55,340 --> 00:17:00,536
Fourth, what does the similarity metric d,
or the causal graph, say about who counts

356
00:17:00,586 --> 00:17:01,236
as comparable?

357
00:17:01,286 --> 00:17:03,334
Individual fairness depends on that
metric.

358
00:17:03,384 --> 00:17:06,132
Counterfactual fairness depends on the
structural causal model.

359
00:17:06,182 --> 00:17:08,580
Both are choices someone has to sign.

360
00:17:08,930 --> 00:17:12,254
Fifth, is a dropped column really gone, or
do proxies remain?

361
00:17:12,304 --> 00:17:15,015
Unawareness removes only the direct use of
an attribute.

362
00:17:15,065 --> 00:17:20,230
A proxy such as zip code can bring it
back, so test the features that are left.

363
00:17:20,580 --> 00:17:25,026
Sixth, who should have decided, and is the
choice written down along with what would

364
00:17:25,076 --> 00:17:25,625
change it?

365
00:17:25,675 --> 00:17:27,124
That is the defended choice.

366
00:17:27,174 --> 00:17:28,323
Then say it exactly.

367
00:17:28,373 --> 00:17:31,620
Not make it fair, but equal error rates,
calibration given up.

368
00:17:32,470 --> 00:17:33,144
Let's recap.

369
00:17:33,194 --> 00:17:36,766
Demographic parity, equalized odds and
calibration parity are different tests.

370
00:17:36,816 --> 00:17:41,111
With unequal base rates, the impossibility
theorem says they cannot all hold.

371
00:17:41,161 --> 00:17:45,819
Each metric carries a values claim, so the
deliverable is a defended choice.

372
00:17:45,869 --> 00:17:49,079
Individual fairness depends entirely on
the similarity metric d.

373
00:17:49,129 --> 00:17:53,786
Dropping a column is unawareness, and a
proxy can bring the attribute back.

374
00:17:53,836 --> 00:17:57,770
The GE Index splits total unfairness into
within-group and between-group parts.

375
00:17:58,120 --> 00:17:59,547
Quiz me on fairness metrics.

376
00:17:59,597 --> 00:18:03,683
Give me one short case at a time, with
made-up numbers for two groups.

377
00:18:03,733 --> 00:18:06,933
I must name the exact term, from
demographic parity, equalized odds,

378
00:18:06,983 --> 00:18:09,888
calibration parity, base rate, proxy,
individual fairness, direct effect and

379
00:18:09,938 --> 00:18:12,842
indirect effect, and say why its closest
look-alike is wrong.

380
00:18:12,892 --> 00:18:15,206
Do not show the answer until I reply.

381
00:18:15,256 --> 00:18:18,160
After six cases, tell me which terms I
mixed up.

382
00:18:18,510 --> 00:18:20,315
Key Terms: Fairness Metrics.

383
00:18:20,365 --> 00:18:22,170
At Nik Bear Brown.
